WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.610 --> 00:00:08.580
Good afternoon.

00:00:08.580 --> 00:00:10.990
Welcome to the
Faculty Forum Online,

00:00:10.990 --> 00:00:14.000
a program of the MIT
Alumni Association

00:00:14.000 --> 00:00:17.930
sponsored in part by the
MIT Professional Education.

00:00:17.930 --> 00:00:20.150
I'm Judy Cole, the
executive vice president

00:00:20.150 --> 00:00:22.700
and CEO of the MIT
Alumni Association,

00:00:22.700 --> 00:00:25.340
and I will serve as
the moderator today.

00:00:25.340 --> 00:00:28.490
If you want to ask
a question, please

00:00:28.490 --> 00:00:31.700
use the box below the
live stream to submit it.

00:00:31.700 --> 00:00:34.100
We will get to as
many as possible.

00:00:34.100 --> 00:00:38.030
Our guest today is Institute
Professor John Harbison,

00:00:38.030 --> 00:00:40.250
a Pulitzer Prize
winning composer

00:00:40.250 --> 00:00:45.560
who joined MIT's music and
theater arts faculty in 1969.

00:00:45.560 --> 00:00:48.860
Professor Harbison has written
for every type of concert

00:00:48.860 --> 00:00:52.190
genre from opera to
intimate solo pieces

00:00:52.190 --> 00:00:55.490
for the Metropolitan Opera,
for the Chicago Symphony,

00:00:55.490 --> 00:00:58.130
for the Boston Symphony,
for New York Philharmonic,

00:00:58.130 --> 00:01:02.720
and the chamber Music Society
of Lincoln Center, among others.

00:01:02.720 --> 00:01:04.700
Here on campus,
Professor Harbison

00:01:04.700 --> 00:01:07.040
founded MIT's
vocal jazz ensemble

00:01:07.040 --> 00:01:11.420
in 2010, for which he served
as the coach and arranger.

00:01:11.420 --> 00:01:15.620
He is a pianist with the faculty
jazz group Strength in Numbers.

00:01:15.620 --> 00:01:18.500
In 2010, the Council
for the Arts at MIT

00:01:18.500 --> 00:01:22.520
awarded Professor
Harbison the Kepes prize,

00:01:22.520 --> 00:01:24.800
presented to a member
of the MIT community

00:01:24.800 --> 00:01:27.680
who has demonstrated excellence
in the creative arts,

00:01:27.680 --> 00:01:31.700
architecture, visual, and
performing arts and writing.

00:01:31.700 --> 00:01:33.530
Professor Harbison, welcome.

00:01:33.530 --> 00:01:35.690
Can you please start
by giving alumni

00:01:35.690 --> 00:01:39.080
an update on your current
and upcoming projects?

00:01:39.080 --> 00:01:40.250
I'll be glad to do that.

00:01:40.250 --> 00:01:46.070
We have a special privilege
today in the broadcast--

00:01:46.070 --> 00:01:49.910
live music, a piece I
wrote in this century--

00:01:49.910 --> 00:01:51.620
early in this century--

00:01:51.620 --> 00:01:55.740
from the collection called
The Violist's Notebook.

00:01:55.740 --> 00:01:59.400
And Marcus Thompson,
Institute Professor,

00:01:59.400 --> 00:02:05.400
my fabulous colleague, great
player, great musician,

00:02:05.400 --> 00:02:08.600
is going to play this
very short piece.

00:02:08.600 --> 00:02:11.970
It's entitled, I guess, or it's
dedicated to Marcus Thompson,

00:02:11.970 --> 00:02:12.869
of all people.

00:02:16.861 --> 00:02:20.354
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:03:14.467 --> 00:03:17.450
[APPLAUSE]

00:03:17.450 --> 00:03:18.890
Thank you, Marcus.

00:03:18.890 --> 00:03:20.424
Thanks.

00:03:20.424 --> 00:03:22.090
It makes a big
difference to have sound.

00:03:22.090 --> 00:03:23.440
It does.

00:03:23.440 --> 00:03:26.470
So now you're going to tell us
about your current projects.

00:03:26.470 --> 00:03:28.450
My current projects are--

00:03:28.450 --> 00:03:36.420
just having recently completed
a piece for a string quartet--

00:03:36.420 --> 00:03:40.000
my sixth string quartet--

00:03:40.000 --> 00:03:45.760
and sixth symphony
about two years ago.

00:03:45.760 --> 00:03:49.480
Coming down the road, I'm
working on a very short opera.

00:03:55.570 --> 00:04:00.530
Well actually, I work
at a slower pace.

00:04:00.530 --> 00:04:03.550
So at this point,
I just finished

00:04:03.550 --> 00:04:07.300
a piece called "Bag
of Tales", which

00:04:07.300 --> 00:04:09.760
is codas-- short
pieces for pianists,

00:04:09.760 --> 00:04:12.220
each dedicated to a
different pianist.

00:04:12.220 --> 00:04:14.650
On Wednesday in New
York, all seven pianists

00:04:14.650 --> 00:04:18.070
will show up and
play their movement.

00:04:18.070 --> 00:04:19.120
This coming Wednesday?

00:04:19.120 --> 00:04:19.720
Yeah.

00:04:19.720 --> 00:04:20.860
Is that open to the public?

00:04:20.860 --> 00:04:21.684
Yes, it is.

00:04:21.684 --> 00:04:23.350
Oh, well tell us where
it's going to be.

00:04:23.350 --> 00:04:24.808
Well, if I could
remember, I would.

00:04:27.400 --> 00:04:28.980
National Sawdust, thank you.

00:04:28.980 --> 00:04:29.680
Marcus knows.

00:04:29.680 --> 00:04:30.995
Thank you.

00:04:30.995 --> 00:04:32.370
All seven pianists
will be there.

00:04:32.370 --> 00:04:34.450
They'll play a bunch
of my piano music,

00:04:34.450 --> 00:04:37.330
and they will play
the seven pieces.

00:04:37.330 --> 00:04:39.220
In fact, I've been
enlisted to play

00:04:39.220 --> 00:04:43.000
one piece from a series called
"Leonard Stein Anagrams."

00:04:43.000 --> 00:04:45.220
So I've been practicing
disproportionately

00:04:45.220 --> 00:04:50.080
large amounts of time trying
to really learn that one minute

00:04:50.080 --> 00:04:52.580
in order to be able to play in
a program with real pianists.

00:04:56.890 --> 00:04:59.110
So thank you for that.

00:04:59.110 --> 00:05:02.330
And a reminder to alumni,
please use the box

00:05:02.330 --> 00:05:04.360
below the live stream
to ask questions

00:05:04.360 --> 00:05:06.010
of Professor Harbison.

00:05:06.010 --> 00:05:08.740
While we wait for the alumni
questions to be submitted,

00:05:08.740 --> 00:05:11.050
I have a question
to start us off.

00:05:11.050 --> 00:05:13.750
Do you consider
music at MIT to be

00:05:13.750 --> 00:05:17.500
as some have called it
MIT's best kept secret?

00:05:17.500 --> 00:05:21.160
Or is the secret
out of the bag now?

00:05:21.160 --> 00:05:24.909
Well, we consider
it out of the bag.

00:05:24.909 --> 00:05:26.950
I know Marcus and I have
talked about this often,

00:05:26.950 --> 00:05:29.410
that we're still surprised
occasionally that people don't

00:05:29.410 --> 00:05:32.140
know how much we're doing here.

00:05:32.140 --> 00:05:34.810
It's partly because
MIT does so much.

00:05:34.810 --> 00:05:37.470
And it's in such a
public way in science

00:05:37.470 --> 00:05:41.320
and engineering
and urban planning.

00:05:41.320 --> 00:05:44.950
It's very newsable subjects.

00:05:44.950 --> 00:05:49.330
I think MIT students come
in here knowing pretty much

00:05:49.330 --> 00:05:50.554
that there is a lot to offer.

00:05:50.554 --> 00:05:51.970
And we know that
a lot of students

00:05:51.970 --> 00:05:55.810
when they have a choice,
students that are qualified

00:05:55.810 --> 00:05:57.910
for a number of
different places,

00:05:57.910 --> 00:05:59.800
they will often tell
us they came here

00:05:59.800 --> 00:06:02.150
because they knew about
what we are doing in music.

00:06:02.150 --> 00:06:05.770
And doesn't some enormous
proportion of the students

00:06:05.770 --> 00:06:08.874
take classes in
music or participate

00:06:08.874 --> 00:06:09.790
in performance groups?

00:06:09.790 --> 00:06:10.990
It's a very large number.

00:06:10.990 --> 00:06:12.650
Yeah, that's what I thought.

00:06:12.650 --> 00:06:20.410
And it seems to always attract
a very high, very well-prepared

00:06:20.410 --> 00:06:21.370
group of students.

00:06:21.370 --> 00:06:24.040
Students who really
know that music

00:06:24.040 --> 00:06:26.240
is going to matter to them.

00:06:26.240 --> 00:06:28.690
Most of them stay with
some aspect of our program

00:06:28.690 --> 00:06:30.230
right on through.

00:06:30.230 --> 00:06:32.830
So yeah, it's a very
satisfying place to work.

00:06:32.830 --> 00:06:35.170
The commitment of a
student here at MIT--

00:06:35.170 --> 00:06:38.050
I suppose it's in
every discipline-- is

00:06:38.050 --> 00:06:38.920
a follow through.

00:06:38.920 --> 00:06:44.100
We get such tremendous
commitment to every project.

00:06:44.100 --> 00:06:45.660
Indeed.

00:06:45.660 --> 00:06:47.310
So a second question
that I have is

00:06:47.310 --> 00:06:49.380
alumni who is studying
under your tutelage

00:06:49.380 --> 00:06:52.680
are now composers themselves
and professors of music

00:06:52.680 --> 00:06:54.840
and professional musicians.

00:06:54.840 --> 00:06:58.720
They are also probably engineers
and physicists as well.

00:06:58.720 --> 00:07:00.900
Can you give us a few
examples of alumni working

00:07:00.900 --> 00:07:03.630
in music who demonstrate the
breadth of careers in music

00:07:03.630 --> 00:07:05.910
among our MIT alumni?

00:07:05.910 --> 00:07:09.764
Well, there are a large number.

00:07:09.764 --> 00:07:15.600
I very recently worked here
with Nina Young and Andrew

00:07:15.600 --> 00:07:21.310
McPherson, both
of them extremely

00:07:21.310 --> 00:07:24.740
distinguished young composers.

00:07:24.740 --> 00:07:27.110
Rome Prize winner
in the case of Nina.

00:07:27.110 --> 00:07:29.000
And Andrew McPherson--
both of them

00:07:29.000 --> 00:07:32.240
Tanglewood Fellows, a very
high distinction also.

00:07:32.240 --> 00:07:36.590
Really competitive with the best
young composers in the country.

00:07:36.590 --> 00:07:37.736
And we expect that.

00:07:37.736 --> 00:07:39.110
We expect that
from time to time.

00:07:39.110 --> 00:07:43.520
We'll have very, very
highly successful musicians

00:07:43.520 --> 00:07:44.630
go into the field.

00:07:44.630 --> 00:07:48.200
They don't necessarily come
here with that firmly in mind,

00:07:48.200 --> 00:07:53.060
but that often seems to
coalesce rather quickly.

00:07:53.060 --> 00:07:54.740
Also in the world
of performance we've

00:07:54.740 --> 00:08:00.790
had first chair players in major
symphonies, and we continue to.

00:08:00.790 --> 00:08:06.040
It's going to be
always, shall we say,

00:08:06.040 --> 00:08:11.060
an in-the-moment
change of career.

00:08:11.060 --> 00:08:12.980
I would have to
really strain to think

00:08:12.980 --> 00:08:15.740
of a student who
came here saying,

00:08:15.740 --> 00:08:18.590
I'm going to be a
professional composer.

00:08:18.590 --> 00:08:21.500
But in the case
of Nina Young, it

00:08:21.500 --> 00:08:24.330
came over her after two
years of graduate school

00:08:24.330 --> 00:08:27.410
in science, that the
musical impulse was too

00:08:27.410 --> 00:08:31.220
strong not to continue.

00:08:31.220 --> 00:08:32.210
Music is like that.

00:08:32.210 --> 00:08:34.640
I mean, if you go into
it as a profession,

00:08:34.640 --> 00:08:39.380
you do it partly because
it takes you over.

00:08:39.380 --> 00:08:41.830
And that's happened
in a number of cases.

00:08:41.830 --> 00:08:44.010
I'm sure that's the case.

00:08:44.010 --> 00:08:46.430
So we have some alumni
questions coming in now,

00:08:46.430 --> 00:08:49.620
so we should take those.

00:08:49.620 --> 00:08:53.630
Dinan in Newton, Mass
asks, whom do you

00:08:53.630 --> 00:08:55.790
consider to be your
greatest collaborator

00:08:55.790 --> 00:08:58.640
either as a musician
or a composer?

00:08:58.640 --> 00:09:00.182
I'm going to add to
that a librettos,

00:09:00.182 --> 00:09:02.431
because I was wondering when
you said you were working

00:09:02.431 --> 00:09:04.070
on an opera, whether or not--

00:09:04.070 --> 00:09:07.580
who do you collaborate
with for the libretto?

00:09:07.580 --> 00:09:10.060
And who else do you still
hope to collaborate with,

00:09:10.060 --> 00:09:12.200
if you had your pick?

00:09:12.200 --> 00:09:13.880
Well, I've done
three operas and I've

00:09:13.880 --> 00:09:17.460
written librettos on my own.

00:09:17.460 --> 00:09:23.060
I do that only for the sake of
collaboration or cooperation.

00:09:23.060 --> 00:09:28.280
I don't want to have
to negotiate too

00:09:28.280 --> 00:09:31.340
frequently about the detail.

00:09:31.340 --> 00:09:34.460
But I've collaborated with some
great performers and composers

00:09:34.460 --> 00:09:35.930
for sure.

00:09:35.930 --> 00:09:39.080
James Levine I would have to
put very high on that list,

00:09:39.080 --> 00:09:44.150
because he is such
an extraordinarily

00:09:44.150 --> 00:09:47.270
complete musician.

00:09:47.270 --> 00:09:52.490
He was so devoted to
a number of my pieces,

00:09:52.490 --> 00:09:56.090
really, over 15, 20 years.

00:09:56.090 --> 00:09:58.430
And I miss him
tremendously in that way

00:09:58.430 --> 00:10:01.850
in that he's restricted his
conducting now pretty much

00:10:01.850 --> 00:10:04.160
to opera at the Met.

00:10:04.160 --> 00:10:07.430
I've worked with a number of
very distinguished singers,

00:10:07.430 --> 00:10:10.190
because of course I've been
always a conductor of Baroque

00:10:10.190 --> 00:10:11.510
music.

00:10:11.510 --> 00:10:14.510
Lorraine Hunt Lieberson, who was
also a very close friend and I

00:10:14.510 --> 00:10:20.360
miss tremendously as a person
of course as much as a singer.

00:10:20.360 --> 00:10:24.270
And all those pianists who are
going to show up on Wednesday

00:10:24.270 --> 00:10:25.500
at National Sawdust.

00:10:25.500 --> 00:10:28.640
I mean, in the
course of a career,

00:10:28.640 --> 00:10:32.120
I think those
collaborations are probably

00:10:32.120 --> 00:10:37.100
the happiest part of it, that
music gets the composer out

00:10:37.100 --> 00:10:40.820
of the studio for sure,
because you have to complete

00:10:40.820 --> 00:10:42.732
the piece with a performer.

00:10:42.732 --> 00:10:43.940
And I've worked with so many.

00:10:43.940 --> 00:10:48.560
Herbert Blomstedt, who
was tremendously committed

00:10:48.560 --> 00:10:50.600
to the idea that an
orchestra piece has

00:10:50.600 --> 00:10:55.430
to come back to the public,
that one round is not enough.

00:10:55.430 --> 00:10:57.080
And he brought back
my Second Symphony

00:10:57.080 --> 00:11:01.100
I think in three seasons in San
Francisco when he was there.

00:11:01.100 --> 00:11:04.390
It's hard to not leave
out great people--

00:11:04.390 --> 00:11:09.320
Dawn Upshaw, of course, from
so early part in her career.

00:11:09.320 --> 00:11:11.440
Still working with her
at Tanglewood in Bach,

00:11:11.440 --> 00:11:14.510
coaching Standy Silva
in a similar way.

00:11:14.510 --> 00:11:16.820
Someone I met very early.

00:11:16.820 --> 00:11:18.680
So many of these people--

00:11:18.680 --> 00:11:22.250
I always say this to
my Tanglewood Fellows--

00:11:22.250 --> 00:11:26.240
these people that you meet at
the beginning of your life,

00:11:26.240 --> 00:11:28.820
when you're all
just starting out--

00:11:28.820 --> 00:11:31.280
for instance with Ursula
Oppens [INAUDIBLE]

00:11:31.280 --> 00:11:34.580
we were starting in
a way, and we still

00:11:34.580 --> 00:11:36.790
are working 30 years later.

00:11:36.790 --> 00:11:39.380
You form a close bond
in those early years.

00:11:39.380 --> 00:11:42.350
So who is left that you
have not yet collaborated

00:11:42.350 --> 00:11:46.400
with that you aspire to?

00:11:46.400 --> 00:11:54.070
Well, I guess I really am
waiting for another conductor

00:11:54.070 --> 00:11:57.285
to make the commitment
that James Levine made

00:11:57.285 --> 00:11:59.770
to me for 20 years.

00:11:59.770 --> 00:12:03.110
It's a rare thing for a composer
to find that in today's world.

00:12:03.110 --> 00:12:06.070
And I had that
huge good fortune.

00:12:06.070 --> 00:12:12.620
And I guess if I dream in
terms or orchestra music,

00:12:12.620 --> 00:12:17.040
I dream of finding again
that kind of a partnership.

00:12:19.920 --> 00:12:22.695
Barbara Hannigan, who
is a great singer who

00:12:22.695 --> 00:12:24.690
I've not worked with--

00:12:24.690 --> 00:12:26.370
every time I hear
her I think, well,

00:12:26.370 --> 00:12:29.070
maybe someday I
will have a chance.

00:12:29.070 --> 00:12:31.530
But I'm happy also
with the great singers

00:12:31.530 --> 00:12:35.730
that I do work with so
regularly at Emmanuel Music

00:12:35.730 --> 00:12:37.800
and at Tanglewood.

00:12:37.800 --> 00:12:42.490
Particularly, starting
careers with people.

00:12:42.490 --> 00:12:45.510
One of the reasons I
loved my recent assignment

00:12:45.510 --> 00:12:49.950
with this Bach Study Weeks at
Tanglewood that I've been doing

00:12:49.950 --> 00:12:52.290
is those singers are
starting their lives.

00:12:52.290 --> 00:12:54.600
And they're starting it
in the music of Bach,

00:12:54.600 --> 00:12:58.210
which is I think the place
to start a life in music.

00:12:58.210 --> 00:13:00.230
Good tip.

00:13:00.230 --> 00:13:03.410
Mikhail in Cambridge
asks, what do you

00:13:03.410 --> 00:13:05.870
think of the current
state of music publishing

00:13:05.870 --> 00:13:09.050
digital copyrights
and their future?

00:13:09.050 --> 00:13:10.775
Music publishing is
still very important,

00:13:10.775 --> 00:13:13.640
but it's extremely
difficult and uphill.

00:13:13.640 --> 00:13:16.490
And I honor and
cherish the people

00:13:16.490 --> 00:13:20.900
that I'm so fortunate to
work with at Schirmer Music

00:13:20.900 --> 00:13:22.370
Sales in New York.

00:13:22.370 --> 00:13:25.730
We still need publishers,
but of course we can't really

00:13:25.730 --> 00:13:28.730
protect any material anymore.

00:13:28.730 --> 00:13:32.090
There's been some good sides
to that the audience perhaps

00:13:32.090 --> 00:13:37.160
has been helped by streaming
and by direct access

00:13:37.160 --> 00:13:39.380
to what we produce.

00:13:39.380 --> 00:13:43.020
On the other hand, the
artistic side of music--

00:13:43.020 --> 00:13:45.170
whether it be pop or concert--

00:13:45.170 --> 00:13:49.320
is now on new footing towards
the public and towards usage.

00:13:49.320 --> 00:13:51.530
And I think that we
have to try to emphasize

00:13:51.530 --> 00:13:54.680
the upside of that, which
is that since people can

00:13:54.680 --> 00:13:58.040
get and use things
with tremendous ease,

00:13:58.040 --> 00:14:01.460
that we really hope that it
plays its role in audience

00:14:01.460 --> 00:14:09.050
building, rather than really
regretting, as we all do

00:14:09.050 --> 00:14:13.010
I'm sure, the loss of income.

00:14:13.010 --> 00:14:15.800
I would imagine it would have
to, because there's simply

00:14:15.800 --> 00:14:18.570
no substitute for seeing
a performance live.

00:14:18.570 --> 00:14:22.010
Seeing it on a screen,
any sort of screen,

00:14:22.010 --> 00:14:23.422
is never going to be--

00:14:23.422 --> 00:14:25.130
you're not going to
have the same energy.

00:14:25.130 --> 00:14:26.380
It is a tremendous difference.

00:14:26.380 --> 00:14:28.460
And that may be one thing
that's being helped.

00:14:28.460 --> 00:14:29.960
The New York
Philharmonic, which was

00:14:29.960 --> 00:14:32.060
one of the toughest
on protecting

00:14:32.060 --> 00:14:34.250
their broadcasts
and their sounds,

00:14:34.250 --> 00:14:36.470
they were the
first big orchestra

00:14:36.470 --> 00:14:38.180
to turn completely around.

00:14:38.180 --> 00:14:41.220
And their audiences have grown.

00:14:41.220 --> 00:14:43.940
So I think there
is something to it.

00:14:43.940 --> 00:14:45.170
It can backfire.

00:14:45.170 --> 00:14:48.800
I really feel sorry
for the Met losing

00:14:48.800 --> 00:14:51.440
a lot in the house
because of the films.

00:14:51.440 --> 00:14:53.840
I know they make a big
effort-- made a big effort

00:14:53.840 --> 00:14:55.520
to reach a bigger public.

00:14:55.520 --> 00:15:00.290
It's sad to go now some days
and see the direct effect

00:15:00.290 --> 00:15:02.010
in the house.

00:15:02.010 --> 00:15:04.220
And a lot of people
who like opera

00:15:04.220 --> 00:15:07.272
are substituting film
for the live performance.

00:15:07.272 --> 00:15:08.730
And of course, the
live performance

00:15:08.730 --> 00:15:10.310
is a fantastic thing.

00:15:10.310 --> 00:15:11.210
Oh, totally.

00:15:11.210 --> 00:15:13.490
It cannot possibly be
duplicated by film.

00:15:13.490 --> 00:15:16.100
When I lived closer to New
York, I used to go to the Met

00:15:16.100 --> 00:15:17.212
on a regular basis.

00:15:17.212 --> 00:15:18.670
So I appreciate
what you're saying.

00:15:18.670 --> 00:15:21.680
There's really nothing
like going to an opera.

00:15:21.680 --> 00:15:26.015
The amount of extraordinary
things that are happening.

00:15:26.015 --> 00:15:28.430
And that's one thing
I think that's been--

00:15:28.430 --> 00:15:31.730
in terms of communication
with the public,

00:15:31.730 --> 00:15:34.010
that it's been
hard to get across.

00:15:34.010 --> 00:15:36.380
Because people think
the films are really

00:15:36.380 --> 00:15:37.404
the experience of opera.

00:15:37.404 --> 00:15:39.320
And of course, if you
go into the opera house,

00:15:39.320 --> 00:15:41.360
you realize they're not.

00:15:41.360 --> 00:15:44.240
I remember from
years ago, someone

00:15:44.240 --> 00:15:47.060
told me-- someone who was in
theater at Carnegie Mellon

00:15:47.060 --> 00:15:50.180
University-- told me
that if someone doesn't

00:15:50.180 --> 00:15:55.220
see live theater before the age
of something like five or 10,

00:15:55.220 --> 00:15:58.120
it's highly likely
that they never will.

00:15:58.120 --> 00:15:58.840
That's very true.

00:15:58.840 --> 00:16:01.340
You need that exposure
early on in life.

00:16:01.340 --> 00:16:03.590
Something happens to you
being in the presence

00:16:03.590 --> 00:16:05.780
of the performer.

00:16:05.780 --> 00:16:08.930
And there's something sonically
that cannot happen in any other

00:16:08.930 --> 00:16:09.680
way.

00:16:09.680 --> 00:16:10.900
Even the sound of voices.

00:16:10.900 --> 00:16:12.470
Yeah, I absolutely believe that.

00:16:12.470 --> 00:16:13.970
And I think that's
one of the things

00:16:13.970 --> 00:16:18.800
that we in the artistic
community need to assert.

00:16:18.800 --> 00:16:20.570
It's actually a
good byproduct, I

00:16:20.570 --> 00:16:24.130
guess, in both the
pop and concert world

00:16:24.130 --> 00:16:27.680
that it's become more
necessary to bring

00:16:27.680 --> 00:16:30.500
the public into the
house, because we don't

00:16:30.500 --> 00:16:32.540
have other income sources.

00:16:32.540 --> 00:16:34.700
Particularly in the
pop world, they've

00:16:34.700 --> 00:16:37.667
now discovered that they must
get out in front of the public.

00:16:37.667 --> 00:16:39.500
I would say, bring the
public into the house

00:16:39.500 --> 00:16:40.710
with their children.

00:16:40.710 --> 00:16:42.230
Which is better.

00:16:42.230 --> 00:16:43.760
It's actually better.

00:16:43.760 --> 00:16:48.170
Todd in Boston asks, you have
said of the venues on campus

00:16:48.170 --> 00:16:50.330
that it is like being
a great football

00:16:50.330 --> 00:16:53.900
institution with small dressing
rooms in a small stadium.

00:16:53.900 --> 00:16:56.670
Do you see that
improving any time soon?

00:16:56.670 --> 00:17:00.650
Well, we do have a real issue
about that in our performance

00:17:00.650 --> 00:17:01.220
spaces.

00:17:01.220 --> 00:17:05.700
And it's been difficult
at the hiring level when

00:17:05.700 --> 00:17:08.630
we show a candidate
for a position

00:17:08.630 --> 00:17:11.869
here what the
performance spaces are,

00:17:11.869 --> 00:17:14.150
we've had people turn us down.

00:17:17.290 --> 00:17:19.260
And of course, we
have great people here

00:17:19.260 --> 00:17:23.000
who are struggling to make
a great program with spaces

00:17:23.000 --> 00:17:25.469
that are not competitive.

00:17:25.469 --> 00:17:27.260
And also, some of us
who go out to a number

00:17:27.260 --> 00:17:30.110
of other institutions to
perform or to take part

00:17:30.110 --> 00:17:34.520
in their programs,
we are struck by how

00:17:34.520 --> 00:17:39.470
really below standard
MIT is in the arts.

00:17:39.470 --> 00:17:43.590
And we've made our case, and
we're continuing to make it.

00:17:43.590 --> 00:17:47.480
Kresge is a great building.

00:17:47.480 --> 00:17:50.000
Famous for good reasons,
and the chapel as well.

00:17:50.000 --> 00:17:53.210
But they were not built
for the performance

00:17:53.210 --> 00:17:57.880
of music, or for theater
performances, for that matter.

00:17:57.880 --> 00:18:02.950
They've been pressed into
service for 70 years.

00:18:02.950 --> 00:18:04.990
And it's not the fault
of the architect,

00:18:04.990 --> 00:18:07.310
because he did what
he was asked to do,

00:18:07.310 --> 00:18:13.000
which is to make a forum
for speech, for lecture.

00:18:13.000 --> 00:18:16.690
So the other spaces are
makeshift and temporary,

00:18:16.690 --> 00:18:20.380
and we regard them as such,
and we do great work in them.

00:18:20.380 --> 00:18:21.850
And our students do great work.

00:18:21.850 --> 00:18:25.249
But I think we are waiting.

00:18:25.249 --> 00:18:27.790
Well, I understand what you're
saying there too, because when

00:18:27.790 --> 00:18:31.090
you try to serve too
many purposes at one time

00:18:31.090 --> 00:18:33.640
or in one facility,
you serve none of them

00:18:33.640 --> 00:18:35.260
as well as could be hoped.

00:18:35.260 --> 00:18:37.420
I think Kresge was built
with the expectation

00:18:37.420 --> 00:18:40.150
that other venues
would take up--

00:18:40.150 --> 00:18:40.810
Would follow.

00:18:40.810 --> 00:18:43.750
--as specialists.

00:18:43.750 --> 00:18:45.690
Because Kresge, I love it.

00:18:45.690 --> 00:18:47.140
I love it more and more.

00:18:47.140 --> 00:18:51.220
I think its brilliance as
a building doesn't fade.

00:18:51.220 --> 00:18:56.080
But its inadequacies for many
of the things that has to do

00:18:56.080 --> 00:18:57.670
remain.

00:18:57.670 --> 00:18:59.590
So going back to
what we were just

00:18:59.590 --> 00:19:01.630
talking about in the
previous question, Nathan

00:19:01.630 --> 00:19:03.950
in Springfield,
Massachusetts asks,

00:19:03.950 --> 00:19:07.630
can you talk about how you
first got into classical music,

00:19:07.630 --> 00:19:10.510
and how you advise parents
to engender that interest

00:19:10.510 --> 00:19:11.950
in their children?

00:19:11.950 --> 00:19:12.700
Well, that was it.

00:19:12.700 --> 00:19:16.900
My parents played on the
piano, and we had a phonograph.

00:19:16.900 --> 00:19:20.950
And I soon learned to pick
out things I wanted to hear.

00:19:20.950 --> 00:19:25.270
And particularly,
my father playing

00:19:25.270 --> 00:19:27.880
versions of Bach
[INAUDIBLE] on the piano.

00:19:27.880 --> 00:19:31.690
That was what got me
going, and playing jazz.

00:19:31.690 --> 00:19:34.910
He was-- I'd not say
a real jazz player,

00:19:34.910 --> 00:19:36.100
but he played the standards.

00:19:36.100 --> 00:19:40.120
And I learned a lot of those
tunes when I was just a kid.

00:19:40.120 --> 00:19:42.130
And I think that's where--

00:19:42.130 --> 00:19:46.060
in the home is where it happens.

00:19:46.060 --> 00:19:49.640
Some people get it just
by foraging around.

00:19:49.640 --> 00:19:52.900
It's hard to do that in a
way, because radio is now

00:19:52.900 --> 00:19:57.610
playing a role that it once
played in bringing music

00:19:57.610 --> 00:20:01.750
to kids, or at least in terms
of bringing concert music

00:20:01.750 --> 00:20:05.320
and jazz and more
esoteric kinds of music.

00:20:05.320 --> 00:20:10.480
So I think the parents, even if
they are not musicians or not

00:20:10.480 --> 00:20:13.360
particular practitioners
at all, if they can just

00:20:13.360 --> 00:20:15.370
show children where it is.

00:20:15.370 --> 00:20:17.650
How do you find it?

00:20:17.650 --> 00:20:20.800
Expose them to it,
and let them hear it.

00:20:20.800 --> 00:20:24.190
Because that's more the
parents than the schools now.

00:20:24.190 --> 00:20:27.820
Schools unfortunately took
quite a hit a long time

00:20:27.820 --> 00:20:31.090
ago in terms of the arts.

00:20:31.090 --> 00:20:33.130
And so it's really at home.

00:20:33.130 --> 00:20:35.770
And then the
wonderful channels--

00:20:35.770 --> 00:20:38.350
there are always channels.

00:20:38.350 --> 00:20:44.350
Places where people can learn
to really become effective

00:20:44.350 --> 00:20:46.090
in these various arts.

00:20:46.090 --> 00:20:48.850
These wonderful youth
orchestras in so many cities,

00:20:48.850 --> 00:20:52.120
which are incredibly
spirited environments,

00:20:52.120 --> 00:20:55.360
and incredibly powerful
in terms of their effect

00:20:55.360 --> 00:20:59.230
on the lives of young musicians.

00:20:59.230 --> 00:21:00.880
Indeed.

00:21:00.880 --> 00:21:03.730
So moving along,
Andrew in Charlotte

00:21:03.730 --> 00:21:07.720
says, can you talk about
a student's piece of music

00:21:07.720 --> 00:21:11.870
that you loved so much that
you listen to it often?

00:21:11.870 --> 00:21:14.540
Yeah, I can.

00:21:14.540 --> 00:21:19.670
Just kind of off the top of
my head, my recent tangle

00:21:19.670 --> 00:21:24.110
with students, we choose them--

00:21:24.110 --> 00:21:27.490
we used to choose them perhaps
for more responsible reasons

00:21:27.490 --> 00:21:28.790
than we came to.

00:21:28.790 --> 00:21:33.070
But now when we do
auditions-- we have 100, 120--

00:21:33.070 --> 00:21:35.920
we would listen to them,
we would bring it down

00:21:35.920 --> 00:21:38.530
to about 20, and we
would pick the six

00:21:38.530 --> 00:21:44.570
that we wanted to listen to for
eight weeks, just as members

00:21:44.570 --> 00:21:46.420
of the crowd, really.

00:21:46.420 --> 00:21:52.310
And in that way, Juri Seo's
piano sonata, I love it.

00:21:52.310 --> 00:21:54.655
I mean, it's crazily
adventurous and peculiar.

00:21:57.170 --> 00:22:00.030
I can remember
quite a few pieces--

00:22:00.030 --> 00:22:03.940
certain pieces of Helen Grime,
a young British composer

00:22:03.940 --> 00:22:08.290
who came to us about eight
years ago at Tanglewood.

00:22:08.290 --> 00:22:10.600
Yeah, of course I grew
up on these pieces,

00:22:10.600 --> 00:22:12.640
because that's
why we chose them.

00:22:12.640 --> 00:22:15.910
We wanted to hear
more of what they did.

00:22:15.910 --> 00:22:20.650
And I think that
finding that criteria

00:22:20.650 --> 00:22:25.360
was a freeing thing
for us as instructors,

00:22:25.360 --> 00:22:29.830
as well as I think just
thinking about the profession.

00:22:29.830 --> 00:22:31.960
What criteria were you
trying to use before you

00:22:31.960 --> 00:22:34.290
came to that conclusion?

00:22:34.290 --> 00:22:40.080
We were trying to in a
way predict something

00:22:40.080 --> 00:22:41.730
about these applicants.

00:22:41.730 --> 00:22:43.320
Predict success of some sort.

00:22:43.320 --> 00:22:46.530
Trying to predict a line
of development we've seen.

00:22:46.530 --> 00:22:52.100
But rather than just being
almost kind of pleasure hounds,

00:22:52.100 --> 00:22:54.990
of what are they
able to do now, that

00:22:54.990 --> 00:22:57.450
proved to be a better vector.

00:22:57.450 --> 00:22:59.930
I can see why.

00:22:59.930 --> 00:23:05.000
So Bonnie in Washington says,
as a former student of yours,

00:23:05.000 --> 00:23:08.220
I'm in awe of all you've done
with your vast repertoire

00:23:08.220 --> 00:23:10.470
and compositions in
the past 20-plus years

00:23:10.470 --> 00:23:12.360
since I took your class.

00:23:12.360 --> 00:23:14.230
Given the recent
political environment,

00:23:14.230 --> 00:23:18.330
including announcements to
consider defunding NEA National

00:23:18.330 --> 00:23:20.250
Endowment for the
Humanities, how

00:23:20.250 --> 00:23:22.140
do you feel
opportunities will be

00:23:22.140 --> 00:23:26.070
impacted with regard to moving
music and the arts forward?

00:23:26.070 --> 00:23:30.150
Well, we've never for
many, many decades

00:23:30.150 --> 00:23:33.319
depended on government
support in the arts.

00:23:33.319 --> 00:23:34.860
The very fact they've
announced again

00:23:34.860 --> 00:23:36.276
and we're going
to defund the arts

00:23:36.276 --> 00:23:38.970
is just a joke, because
compared to other countries that

00:23:38.970 --> 00:23:43.260
are serious about this,
we're not in the game.

00:23:43.260 --> 00:23:46.920
So really what it's about
is these old systems

00:23:46.920 --> 00:23:50.970
that we've always had, which
is really private patronage.

00:23:50.970 --> 00:23:55.080
And we need to
embrace and celebrate

00:23:55.080 --> 00:23:58.510
people who are game enough
and adventurous enough

00:23:58.510 --> 00:24:01.755
to participate in that.

00:24:01.755 --> 00:24:05.040
It can be an exciting
and stimulating thing

00:24:05.040 --> 00:24:07.140
for people to be
interested enough

00:24:07.140 --> 00:24:11.730
in the arts to want to
really step up and make

00:24:11.730 --> 00:24:13.890
sure they keep going.

00:24:13.890 --> 00:24:17.880
And I'm encouraged that
there's a lot of signs

00:24:17.880 --> 00:24:20.590
of vitality within that system.

00:24:20.590 --> 00:24:24.120
We can't look to any
other systems of the kind

00:24:24.120 --> 00:24:29.640
that Germany and France and
England have made work so well.

00:24:29.640 --> 00:24:32.340
We're just not in
that frame of mind.

00:24:32.340 --> 00:24:36.030
They look at our
tradition-- it really

00:24:36.030 --> 00:24:39.180
is a tradition of
philanthropy in the arts-- as

00:24:39.180 --> 00:24:40.110
somehow corrupt.

00:24:40.110 --> 00:24:42.570
But I look at the government
system over there as corrupt.

00:24:45.612 --> 00:24:49.530
It's all a social network, which
produces hopefully occasionally

00:24:49.530 --> 00:24:53.290
some really worthy, good things.

00:24:53.290 --> 00:24:55.440
I'm just writing a piece
for Chanticleer, which

00:24:55.440 --> 00:24:57.920
is a vocal group in California.

00:24:57.920 --> 00:25:02.400
Now, Chanticleer has existed
for now probably 30, 40 years,

00:25:02.400 --> 00:25:04.530
as an all-male choir.

00:25:04.530 --> 00:25:10.770
An unlikely thing, but supported
locally from the San Francisco

00:25:10.770 --> 00:25:13.050
area with amazing following.

00:25:13.050 --> 00:25:15.940
And by concert music
standards, they're

00:25:15.940 --> 00:25:17.910
a really popular audience.

00:25:17.910 --> 00:25:20.110
This is doable.

00:25:20.110 --> 00:25:23.792
So I just read the other day
the big announcement, well,

00:25:23.792 --> 00:25:25.500
we're not going to
fund the arts anymore.

00:25:25.500 --> 00:25:28.167
Well, that's just
completely mirrors.

00:25:28.167 --> 00:25:29.250
You're not funding it now.

00:25:29.250 --> 00:25:32.624
That's complete nonsense.

00:25:32.624 --> 00:25:36.630
So Maxine in Boston says,
I majored in engineering,

00:25:36.630 --> 00:25:40.140
but I minored in music
at MIT, and I loved it.

00:25:40.140 --> 00:25:42.720
I am a classically
trained pianist.

00:25:42.720 --> 00:25:44.220
Are there musical
groups available

00:25:44.220 --> 00:25:46.410
for alumni like me to join?

00:25:46.410 --> 00:25:50.220
Yes, actually I can't
give specific references,

00:25:50.220 --> 00:25:54.180
but I know in two
cities in San Francisco

00:25:54.180 --> 00:25:59.700
and in Minneapolis there were
MIT music network groups.

00:25:59.700 --> 00:26:00.810
So she's here in Boston.

00:26:00.810 --> 00:26:02.726
Do we have one in Boston
that you're aware of?

00:26:02.726 --> 00:26:04.100
Oh, there must be one in Boston.

00:26:04.100 --> 00:26:05.630
Informally, the
Longwood Orchestra

00:26:05.630 --> 00:26:12.060
has long been a kind
of MIT-inhabited group.

00:26:12.060 --> 00:26:14.010
A lot of people who
are premeds here

00:26:14.010 --> 00:26:16.440
who have gone and
keep on playing.

00:26:16.440 --> 00:26:20.950
There must be in Boston.

00:26:20.950 --> 00:26:24.150
There are a lot of smaller
musical groups in Boston,

00:26:24.150 --> 00:26:24.660
it seems.

00:26:24.660 --> 00:26:28.020
I am always seeing announcements
about their concerts.

00:26:28.020 --> 00:26:29.970
But I was always
impressed by the vitality

00:26:29.970 --> 00:26:33.530
of the Sanfrancisco
MIT musical network.

00:26:33.530 --> 00:26:36.930
It seemed to go
generation to generation.

00:26:36.930 --> 00:26:40.620
Jeevan in Miami asks an
interesting question.

00:26:40.620 --> 00:26:43.500
Where does inspiration
come from for you?

00:26:43.500 --> 00:26:47.400
Do you see music and art as
more in the act of creating

00:26:47.400 --> 00:26:50.140
or in the act of performing?

00:26:50.140 --> 00:26:51.005
Well--

00:26:51.005 --> 00:26:55.770
How do you tease
those apart, actually?

00:26:55.770 --> 00:26:57.630
I think what I've
learned about inspiration

00:26:57.630 --> 00:27:04.500
is that you don't disdain
even the most fragmentary sort

00:27:04.500 --> 00:27:10.090
of indication of a direction.

00:27:10.090 --> 00:27:14.070
I learned to write
down so I remember

00:27:14.070 --> 00:27:18.300
even little fragments that
seemed to have no potential.

00:27:18.300 --> 00:27:23.130
Because I work always on
the basis of sending out

00:27:23.130 --> 00:27:29.230
a runner if I have anything
like a melody or a chord

00:27:29.230 --> 00:27:35.270
that will run out ahead
and show direction.

00:27:35.270 --> 00:27:40.250
I just learned to value the
unconscious and the things that

00:27:40.250 --> 00:27:44.480
don't seem to be
potentially useful.

00:27:44.480 --> 00:27:46.850
I get as much out of the
not potentially useful

00:27:46.850 --> 00:27:50.630
as the things that I'm
quite sure about right away.

00:27:50.630 --> 00:27:53.390
And I think that
pragmatism is something

00:27:53.390 --> 00:27:57.040
that I try to suggest to
the Tanglewood Fellows I

00:27:57.040 --> 00:28:00.100
teach who are so highly gifted.

00:28:00.100 --> 00:28:06.520
But that's where the
independent side creeps in,

00:28:06.520 --> 00:28:11.020
is if you begin to respect
the things that don't fit.

00:28:11.020 --> 00:28:13.770
That makes a lot of sense.

00:28:13.770 --> 00:28:16.620
Ken in Chicago
says, can you talk

00:28:16.620 --> 00:28:18.960
about a commission
you've turned down

00:28:18.960 --> 00:28:21.360
or work you have
abandoned, something

00:28:21.360 --> 00:28:23.460
that has challenged you
are still haunts you

00:28:23.460 --> 00:28:26.230
for leaving behind?

00:28:26.230 --> 00:28:30.970
Well, I've turned down a
couple that I eventually

00:28:30.970 --> 00:28:34.900
did that I feel lucky
that I changed my mind.

00:28:34.900 --> 00:28:36.580
Why did you turn
them down initially,

00:28:36.580 --> 00:28:38.380
and what turned you around?

00:28:38.380 --> 00:28:43.540
I turned down the commission
from the Chicago Consulate

00:28:43.540 --> 00:28:47.340
of the State of Israel
for the 50th anniversary

00:28:47.340 --> 00:28:49.844
of the establishment of
Israel, which eventually turned

00:28:49.844 --> 00:28:51.260
into a piece called
"Four Psalms",

00:28:51.260 --> 00:28:53.620
which I think is one
of my best pieces.

00:28:53.620 --> 00:28:56.320
I turned it down for
a number of reasons.

00:28:56.320 --> 00:28:58.940
I was pressing towards
completing opera

00:28:58.940 --> 00:28:59.680
at Great Gatsby.

00:28:59.680 --> 00:29:01.450
I didn't think I had time.

00:29:01.450 --> 00:29:03.430
And I was a little
worried that they

00:29:03.430 --> 00:29:07.780
had chosen a non-Jewish composer
for this really big commission,

00:29:07.780 --> 00:29:10.600
though the commissioning
body seemed

00:29:10.600 --> 00:29:13.090
quite confident that that's
what they wanted to do.

00:29:13.090 --> 00:29:17.749
And I called the Consul
General when I changed my mind.

00:29:17.749 --> 00:29:18.790
I said, I'm just haunted.

00:29:18.790 --> 00:29:20.680
It was a couple of months later.

00:29:20.680 --> 00:29:23.830
Is there any chance I
could still get into this?

00:29:23.830 --> 00:29:27.880
He said, well, yes, it's awkward
and shame on you, and so forth.

00:29:27.880 --> 00:29:32.920
But I felt incredibly
fortunate to get back

00:29:32.920 --> 00:29:34.990
into the project, because
it had haunted me.

00:29:34.990 --> 00:29:37.090
I thought, this
was an opportunity

00:29:37.090 --> 00:29:38.650
first of all for study.

00:29:38.650 --> 00:29:41.560
Sometimes the research
behind a piece

00:29:41.560 --> 00:29:44.890
is as valuable a part of
the experience as anything,

00:29:44.890 --> 00:29:47.560
that you have to
learn so much to just

00:29:47.560 --> 00:29:51.250
be able to grasp what
the piece could be about.

00:29:51.250 --> 00:29:52.900
And in that case my studying--

00:29:52.900 --> 00:29:55.920
I studied very beginning Hebrew.

00:29:55.920 --> 00:29:58.120
I knew I wanted to work
in Hebrew, some of it.

00:29:58.120 --> 00:30:04.390
And I went to Israel for a
two or three week period.

00:30:04.390 --> 00:30:10.630
It was an incredibly
irreplaceable enlargement

00:30:10.630 --> 00:30:13.060
of my experience.

00:30:13.060 --> 00:30:15.490
And I think that's
the kind of thing--

00:30:15.490 --> 00:30:17.500
I guess about the
things I've turned down,

00:30:17.500 --> 00:30:20.440
I usually try to say, well,
it wasn't salvageable.

00:30:20.440 --> 00:30:22.060
I don't look back on that.

00:30:22.060 --> 00:30:26.030
But more than that time,
I've been haunted and been

00:30:26.030 --> 00:30:28.940
able to save the project.

00:30:28.940 --> 00:30:31.390
Well, the fact that
ultimately came around

00:30:31.390 --> 00:30:33.530
says to me that it
was meant to be.

00:30:33.530 --> 00:30:34.570
Yeah, I don't know.

00:30:34.570 --> 00:30:35.920
It meant that the group--

00:30:35.920 --> 00:30:37.960
which I discovered later--

00:30:37.960 --> 00:30:41.660
the commissioning group in
Chicago were very engaged.

00:30:41.660 --> 00:30:44.470
In fact, when I submitted
my text, every one of them--

00:30:44.470 --> 00:30:47.470
every element of the
text was objected to

00:30:47.470 --> 00:30:49.260
by the commissioners.

00:30:49.260 --> 00:30:52.440
And eventually, the Consul
General stepped in and said,

00:30:52.440 --> 00:30:55.170
we hired this
composer, and we better

00:30:55.170 --> 00:30:57.270
respect what he wants to do.

00:30:57.270 --> 00:31:01.380
And so I did it,
and I'm really--

00:31:01.380 --> 00:31:04.530
I hope that piece makes a few
more appearances, because I

00:31:04.530 --> 00:31:08.430
think [? unsadly ?] enough,
it's still as relevant

00:31:08.430 --> 00:31:12.690
now as it was a long time ago.

00:31:16.610 --> 00:31:18.480
Another alumnus question.

00:31:18.480 --> 00:31:21.330
Jim in St. Louis
says, regarding radio,

00:31:21.330 --> 00:31:23.580
I hear less and
less classical music

00:31:23.580 --> 00:31:25.920
on the radio where I
live, and have resorted

00:31:25.920 --> 00:31:27.870
to podcasts and YouTube.

00:31:27.870 --> 00:31:29.940
But I'm my own DJ there.

00:31:29.940 --> 00:31:31.530
What else do you
recommend for keeping

00:31:31.530 --> 00:31:35.790
abreast of contemporary
composers online or on air?

00:31:35.790 --> 00:31:38.850
Yeah, and radio
is a big loss now.

00:31:38.850 --> 00:31:43.500
We learned so much about what
is going on in music from radio,

00:31:43.500 --> 00:31:47.160
going way back to
when I was very young.

00:31:47.160 --> 00:31:50.250
And I got my job as a
composer in residence

00:31:50.250 --> 00:31:53.310
in Pittsburgh symphony
because of radio.

00:31:53.310 --> 00:31:56.632
A piece of mine came on
Andre Previn's car radio,

00:31:56.632 --> 00:31:58.340
and he pulled off to
the side of the road

00:31:58.340 --> 00:32:00.410
and waited to get an ID.

00:32:00.410 --> 00:32:03.620
And I wound up
going to Pittsburgh.

00:32:03.620 --> 00:32:05.390
And that was when
radio was still

00:32:05.390 --> 00:32:08.420
a life force in
all kinds of music,

00:32:08.420 --> 00:32:14.000
and even jazz has lost a huge
amount of ground on radio.

00:32:14.000 --> 00:32:17.630
So I think the
substitute source is

00:32:17.630 --> 00:32:25.070
the websites of composers of all
kinds, both pop and in concert,

00:32:25.070 --> 00:32:29.180
where sometimes entire
pieces are available.

00:32:29.180 --> 00:32:33.230
Publishers of composers, that's
become the substitute venue

00:32:33.230 --> 00:32:35.580
for finding out about music.

00:32:35.580 --> 00:32:38.870
And sadly, you have to kind of
know what you're looking for.

00:32:38.870 --> 00:32:42.830
What about the websites of some
of the famous classical music

00:32:42.830 --> 00:32:46.190
stations in Boston and New
York and other major cities,

00:32:46.190 --> 00:32:47.850
like WGBH?

00:32:47.850 --> 00:32:49.380
Yeah, I think that's helpful.

00:32:49.380 --> 00:32:51.780
But it's not nearly as
good as what we did have,

00:32:51.780 --> 00:32:55.070
which was the ideas of
some programmer who really

00:32:55.070 --> 00:32:56.810
put together a broadcast.

00:32:56.810 --> 00:32:59.330
I'd say that the
undergraduate radio

00:32:59.330 --> 00:33:01.340
stations, the college
radio stations,

00:33:01.340 --> 00:33:04.910
are doing best,
certainly in this city.

00:33:04.910 --> 00:33:09.350
HRB still hangs in well
at Harvard Emerson.

00:33:09.350 --> 00:33:11.930
That's our best hope,
because there you have

00:33:11.930 --> 00:33:14.480
an enterprising junior who--

00:33:14.480 --> 00:33:17.420
a couple of years ago I
heard a great broadcast

00:33:17.420 --> 00:33:22.010
on the bass player Ray
Brown by a jazz hound

00:33:22.010 --> 00:33:25.430
who knew everything Ray
Brown ever recorded.

00:33:25.430 --> 00:33:30.850
And they get an hour and
put on a great broadcast.

00:33:30.850 --> 00:33:34.940
That still can happen, because
so much initiative there.

00:33:34.940 --> 00:33:38.510
But in general, we don't have--

00:33:38.510 --> 00:33:45.860
most radio is very little
dedicated to music of any kind.

00:33:45.860 --> 00:33:49.700
Toby in Singapore says,
I am grateful to you

00:33:49.700 --> 00:33:51.440
for being my chamber
music professor

00:33:51.440 --> 00:33:53.930
when I played cello at MIT.

00:33:53.930 --> 00:33:56.450
My six-year-old son is
now learning about music

00:33:56.450 --> 00:33:59.650
from one of your composition
students John Sharpley.

00:33:59.650 --> 00:34:00.350
Indeed.

00:34:00.350 --> 00:34:02.820
Can you talk about when
you developed your interest

00:34:02.820 --> 00:34:06.140
in composition, and how you
were encouraged to develop

00:34:06.140 --> 00:34:08.570
your musical writing style?

00:34:08.570 --> 00:34:09.659
Well, I started young.

00:34:09.659 --> 00:34:11.870
A lot of composers do.

00:34:11.870 --> 00:34:15.830
And I had written pieces
at a very, very small age,

00:34:15.830 --> 00:34:20.570
like five, six, seven years old.

00:34:20.570 --> 00:34:24.560
And I had a teacher
in high school

00:34:24.560 --> 00:34:26.905
who was my piano teacher,
but she didn't like

00:34:26.905 --> 00:34:28.030
the way I played the piano.

00:34:28.030 --> 00:34:29.719
I was already a
jazz player, and she

00:34:29.719 --> 00:34:32.600
didn't want to listen to
me swinging Beethoven.

00:34:32.600 --> 00:34:34.770
So she looked at my pieces then.

00:34:34.770 --> 00:34:41.020
And so I got a lot of support
just by saying, here it is.

00:34:41.020 --> 00:34:43.790
I'm doing this.

00:34:43.790 --> 00:34:48.230
And I had the advantage of
some quite interested teachers.

00:34:48.230 --> 00:34:52.280
I think again, this
is a good situation

00:34:52.280 --> 00:34:56.389
where the parents and
children can work together

00:34:56.389 --> 00:34:57.950
at that first stage.

00:34:57.950 --> 00:35:01.190
Over and over as I look at
biographies of composers

00:35:01.190 --> 00:35:05.825
who turned into really
productive practitioners,

00:35:05.825 --> 00:35:09.740
a high percentage had
that parent at the start,

00:35:09.740 --> 00:35:12.290
right in the midst of it.

00:35:12.290 --> 00:35:20.080
And really, that kind of
early help, first of all,

00:35:20.080 --> 00:35:22.670
it makes you feel less weird.

00:35:22.670 --> 00:35:28.940
Because socially, those kinds of
interests are hard to fit into.

00:35:28.940 --> 00:35:31.159
They tend not to be
the mainstream fad

00:35:31.159 --> 00:35:31.950
kinds of interests.

00:35:31.950 --> 00:35:34.190
Indeed I regretted
for many years,

00:35:34.190 --> 00:35:40.790
once I was invited to play
in the high school assembly.

00:35:40.790 --> 00:35:44.750
And they said, play anything
you want for high school.

00:35:44.750 --> 00:35:46.860
So I played the pieces
I was working on,

00:35:46.860 --> 00:35:51.260
which were the Schoenberg
Piano pieces Opus 11.

00:35:51.260 --> 00:35:56.090
And all the girls I was
interested in, they ran away.

00:35:56.090 --> 00:36:00.490
And my friends didn't invite
me out for a long time.

00:36:00.490 --> 00:36:02.300
And I was I guess very naive.

00:36:02.300 --> 00:36:05.660
I thought, this is
interesting to me.

00:36:05.660 --> 00:36:11.880
But because one is as a young
musician, even a jazzer now,

00:36:11.880 --> 00:36:14.690
is somewhat isolated by
the intensity of what

00:36:14.690 --> 00:36:19.160
they're doing, that parental
support is very helpful--

00:36:19.160 --> 00:36:20.780
parental and teacher
support, I guess,

00:36:20.780 --> 00:36:25.790
because you do take a
different social track.

00:36:25.790 --> 00:36:28.850
I think that's probably
true of any passion

00:36:28.850 --> 00:36:32.274
that a student pursues as
intensely as most of our MIT

00:36:32.274 --> 00:36:33.565
students pursue their passions.

00:36:33.565 --> 00:36:34.117
Absolutely.

00:36:34.117 --> 00:36:35.200
That's what I see in them.

00:36:35.200 --> 00:36:37.280
And that may be what
they find in each other

00:36:37.280 --> 00:36:38.270
when they come here.

00:36:38.270 --> 00:36:39.710
I see that in MIT students.

00:36:39.710 --> 00:36:41.330
I recognize it.

00:36:41.330 --> 00:36:46.700
And some of them have been
through socially awkward high

00:36:46.700 --> 00:36:49.040
school experiences
because they're so

00:36:49.040 --> 00:36:50.810
wrapped up in something.

00:36:50.810 --> 00:36:52.730
And for me, it was
concert music, which

00:36:52.730 --> 00:36:55.010
was a fairly rare passion.

00:36:55.010 --> 00:36:58.040
The jazz helped, but
jazz already by that time

00:36:58.040 --> 00:37:01.340
was becoming another
form of rather

00:37:01.340 --> 00:37:03.650
specialized appreciation.

00:37:03.650 --> 00:37:09.140
So yes, it was great in
college to meet some more

00:37:09.140 --> 00:37:10.370
people that were like that.

00:37:10.370 --> 00:37:15.140
And I think MIT is an
unbelievable meeting

00:37:15.140 --> 00:37:20.040
place for people who've been
nurturing extraordinary project

00:37:20.040 --> 00:37:22.970
on their own.

00:37:22.970 --> 00:37:25.850
Jill in Brookline says,
speaking of Kresge,

00:37:25.850 --> 00:37:29.210
it was built with a
pipe organ built in.

00:37:29.210 --> 00:37:34.240
So perhaps the design for music
was just not up to the goals.

00:37:34.240 --> 00:37:36.700
Well, the pipe organ was part
of the original commission.

00:37:36.700 --> 00:37:41.990
And it was because it was being
envisioned as a public events

00:37:41.990 --> 00:37:43.720
site for MIT.

00:37:43.720 --> 00:37:48.110
The pipe organ makes me sad to
hear our questioner mention it,

00:37:48.110 --> 00:37:51.410
because of course it
was a major instrument,

00:37:51.410 --> 00:37:55.220
as was the one in the chapel.

00:37:55.220 --> 00:37:56.450
Both are in disrepair.

00:37:56.450 --> 00:37:59.550
And organ repair
is so expensive,

00:37:59.550 --> 00:38:02.450
it's probably far down
on the to-do list.

00:38:02.450 --> 00:38:05.750
But the reason for that great
Kresge organ being put there

00:38:05.750 --> 00:38:10.580
was that the sort of
great state occasions that

00:38:10.580 --> 00:38:13.625
are part of a big university
were going to occur there.

00:38:13.625 --> 00:38:14.840
You need triumphal music.

00:38:14.840 --> 00:38:18.620
Now there isn't much on
that organ that we can use.

00:38:18.620 --> 00:38:23.500
And both the MIT
organs are awaiting

00:38:23.500 --> 00:38:27.320
a very unusual
and perhaps crazed

00:38:27.320 --> 00:38:33.170
kind of benefactor, a deeply
organ-loving benefactor.

00:38:33.170 --> 00:38:36.560
Because they are among the
great instruments in the city.

00:38:36.560 --> 00:38:39.320
And of course at this
point, they're unheard.

00:38:39.320 --> 00:38:43.670
But they speak of another
era of university life,

00:38:43.670 --> 00:38:48.530
where I guess that kind
of ceremonial conclave

00:38:48.530 --> 00:38:51.860
is less frequent.

00:38:51.860 --> 00:38:56.450
But the Kresgy organ, before
I was a faculty member here,

00:38:56.450 --> 00:38:57.530
I heard it.

00:38:57.530 --> 00:39:01.340
And it was one of the big
grand organs of the region.

00:39:04.100 --> 00:39:07.730
Mason in Dartmouth,
New Hampshire says,

00:39:07.730 --> 00:39:10.880
your work testifies to
your love of poetry, e.g.

00:39:10.880 --> 00:39:13.940
The arrangement of Louis
Gluck's The Seven Ages.

00:39:13.940 --> 00:39:16.910
Can you tell us more about
how you incorporate poetry

00:39:16.910 --> 00:39:19.640
into your process
when composing?

00:39:19.640 --> 00:39:24.720
That's my main ancillary reading
is poetry, more than novels.

00:39:24.720 --> 00:39:32.020
And I do like short stories,
but novel reading, it's too time

00:39:32.020 --> 00:39:33.530
spanned a thing for me.

00:39:33.530 --> 00:39:35.120
Poetry I read all the time.

00:39:35.120 --> 00:39:40.550
And I get the Poetry A Day thing
from the Poetry Foundation,

00:39:40.550 --> 00:39:43.850
and I get Poetry Magazine.

00:39:43.850 --> 00:39:47.240
I'm like someone who comes
into the Grolier bookstore,

00:39:47.240 --> 00:39:50.600
and they say, you don't
seem to be a poet.

00:39:50.600 --> 00:39:53.720
What are you doing here?

00:39:53.720 --> 00:39:58.160
And poetry means a
lot, because it sort of

00:39:58.160 --> 00:40:02.790
brings in the kind of world I
want to be in when I'm working.

00:40:02.790 --> 00:40:05.820
And I have felt
the collaboration

00:40:05.820 --> 00:40:12.840
of poets dead or alive to be a
huge changing force in my life.

00:40:12.840 --> 00:40:15.990
In the Louis Gluck case,
I've had the great fortune

00:40:15.990 --> 00:40:18.720
of after setting her,
becoming a friend of hers,

00:40:18.720 --> 00:40:20.790
and really
understanding something

00:40:20.790 --> 00:40:28.920
about her off-work world,
which is meaningful to me,

00:40:28.920 --> 00:40:33.920
and for which I feel kinship.

00:40:33.920 --> 00:40:35.540
Wow.

00:40:35.540 --> 00:40:39.740
Larry in Scituate, Mass
says, Lloyd Schwartz

00:40:39.740 --> 00:40:41.960
called Gabriella
Diaz's performance

00:40:41.960 --> 00:40:45.290
if your "For Violin
Alone" in December one

00:40:45.290 --> 00:40:47.450
of the highlights of the year.

00:40:47.450 --> 00:40:49.640
What are your impressions
of how this piece has

00:40:49.640 --> 00:40:51.200
been interpreted?

00:40:51.200 --> 00:40:53.360
Well, that was a very
wonderful occasion for me.

00:40:53.360 --> 00:40:56.850
That piece has a
strange history.

00:40:56.850 --> 00:41:00.620
I'm not quite sure why, but
the commissioner of the piece,

00:41:00.620 --> 00:41:03.080
Jennifer Koh-- a very
distinguished violinist--

00:41:03.080 --> 00:41:07.060
played it four times, never
making a recording of it

00:41:07.060 --> 00:41:08.540
or really commenting
on the piece.

00:41:08.540 --> 00:41:11.180
So I had never really
heard the piece

00:41:11.180 --> 00:41:17.060
until two friends, Daniel
Stepner and Gabby Diaz,

00:41:17.060 --> 00:41:19.530
both did the piece this season.

00:41:19.530 --> 00:41:21.470
And so that's my
first acquaintance

00:41:21.470 --> 00:41:25.110
with the piece by then
four or five years back.

00:41:25.110 --> 00:41:27.500
I was tremendously glad
that both of those players

00:41:27.500 --> 00:41:28.550
took the piece on.

00:41:28.550 --> 00:41:32.494
Because it's a large piece, and
it's a big chunk of one's life.

00:41:32.494 --> 00:41:34.160
When you're a composer,
when you haven't

00:41:34.160 --> 00:41:38.330
met the piece in the
real in the room,

00:41:38.330 --> 00:41:42.020
you don't have a complete
relationship to it.

00:41:42.020 --> 00:41:45.050
I mean, obviously you should
know what you did more or less.

00:41:45.050 --> 00:41:49.370
But you don't have the
rest of the equation,

00:41:49.370 --> 00:41:53.510
like what happens when
it's in a room with people.

00:41:53.510 --> 00:41:56.660
I used to be annoyed when
a couple of my colleagues--

00:41:56.660 --> 00:41:59.992
older, in those days, my
experienced colleagues-- said

00:41:59.992 --> 00:42:01.950
you listen to the way
people are breathing when

00:42:01.950 --> 00:42:03.075
your piece is being played.

00:42:03.075 --> 00:42:05.180
You listen to the coughs.

00:42:05.180 --> 00:42:09.230
These are all indicative
of how you are holding them

00:42:09.230 --> 00:42:11.070
for that amount of time.

00:42:11.070 --> 00:42:13.160
I didn't take it
seriously at the time.

00:42:13.160 --> 00:42:14.300
I do now.

00:42:14.300 --> 00:42:18.860
And that's part of
what completes a piece.

00:42:18.860 --> 00:42:20.960
And certainly, that
occasion with Gabby

00:42:20.960 --> 00:42:24.720
playing and also within Dan
a few weeks before that,

00:42:24.720 --> 00:42:32.420
I managed to get that last
sort of lap with that piece.

00:42:32.420 --> 00:42:36.530
A little bit of
relaxation occurred too,

00:42:36.530 --> 00:42:39.050
because you evaluate sort
of how close does it come up

00:42:39.050 --> 00:42:42.620
to what you were trying to do?

00:42:42.620 --> 00:42:45.516
Yeah, I can see
where the creation

00:42:45.516 --> 00:42:47.140
of a piece, the
composition of a piece,

00:42:47.140 --> 00:42:49.639
and then the performance of the
piece or sort of intricately

00:42:49.639 --> 00:42:53.290
intertwined in terms
of that whole process.

00:42:53.290 --> 00:42:57.830
But the performance is
the other end of it.

00:42:57.830 --> 00:43:00.790
And as much as we don't worry
about that when we're writing

00:43:00.790 --> 00:43:03.970
that piece-- because that would
be inhibiting in terms of what

00:43:03.970 --> 00:43:06.040
you want to try in the piece--

00:43:06.040 --> 00:43:11.230
at the point when it's done,
you're waiting for a circle

00:43:11.230 --> 00:43:13.060
to close.

00:43:13.060 --> 00:43:16.070
So at the grave risk of
running over time a little bit,

00:43:16.070 --> 00:43:19.270
we are going to take one
last question from Don,

00:43:19.270 --> 00:43:21.910
class of '61, Boothbay, Maine.

00:43:21.910 --> 00:43:25.000
He says, I'm an old grad
who is writing music--

00:43:25.000 --> 00:43:28.510
chamber music, choral music,
songs, and five operas.

00:43:28.510 --> 00:43:30.220
It would be marvelous
to hear some

00:43:30.220 --> 00:43:31.990
of my work performed in Kresge.

00:43:31.990 --> 00:43:35.840
Is there any chance of that?

00:43:35.840 --> 00:43:41.160
Well, you do what composers
do when you write pieces.

00:43:41.160 --> 00:43:46.070
You try to get
immediate colleagues who

00:43:46.070 --> 00:43:49.895
you can trust to
really work on them

00:43:49.895 --> 00:43:54.410
and to provide some sonic
material that you can send out.

00:43:54.410 --> 00:43:55.760
Or you can send out scores.

00:43:55.760 --> 00:43:58.820
People are not as energetic
at really studying scores

00:43:58.820 --> 00:44:04.470
as they once were, because
the sound material is easier

00:44:04.470 --> 00:44:07.260
to get at.

00:44:07.260 --> 00:44:10.280
But that's what
composers all do.

00:44:10.280 --> 00:44:13.760
And what I always say to--

00:44:13.760 --> 00:44:16.590
this is very obviously a
veteran musician here--

00:44:16.590 --> 00:44:18.260
what I would say to
the young composers

00:44:18.260 --> 00:44:21.920
is that you have to get
a community around you,

00:44:21.920 --> 00:44:24.290
a few people who will do
some work on the pieces,

00:44:24.290 --> 00:44:26.400
so that you can make that
equation we were just

00:44:26.400 --> 00:44:28.470
talking about.

00:44:28.470 --> 00:44:33.980
And it's from that base
that you expand outwards.

00:44:33.980 --> 00:44:38.450
And it would seem that
this questioner must

00:44:38.450 --> 00:44:41.930
have someone or some
people around who will say,

00:44:41.930 --> 00:44:43.670
I'll take this piece.

00:44:43.670 --> 00:44:45.070
I'll learn it.

00:44:45.070 --> 00:44:47.150
We'll get a really
good version of it,

00:44:47.150 --> 00:44:49.070
and we'll get people interested.

00:44:49.070 --> 00:44:53.290
Do composers pitch directly to
performance groups sometimes?

00:44:53.290 --> 00:44:54.715
They do.

00:44:54.715 --> 00:44:57.210
And I find that
the most effective

00:44:57.210 --> 00:45:00.080
is the fewer people you're
pitching to, the better.

00:45:00.080 --> 00:45:02.030
The easier it is to sell them.

00:45:02.030 --> 00:45:05.420
Well, what I was
thinking is, look up

00:45:05.420 --> 00:45:07.880
some of the student
groups on campus.

00:45:07.880 --> 00:45:10.430
Go to a student group and
see if they'll take it on.

00:45:10.430 --> 00:45:10.960
Absolutely.

00:45:10.960 --> 00:45:17.240
And of course, here at MIT with
some of the student ensembles,

00:45:17.240 --> 00:45:20.990
we do compositions by
their colleagues, which

00:45:20.990 --> 00:45:24.440
is the really logical equation.

00:45:24.440 --> 00:45:28.900
But I think of the people
that I gave pieces to

00:45:28.900 --> 00:45:31.150
or that asked for pieces
when I was just starting out,

00:45:31.150 --> 00:45:34.210
Ursula Oppens, And I'm
still with people like that.

00:45:34.210 --> 00:45:36.260
Bob Levine.

00:45:36.260 --> 00:45:37.730
It's been-- how many?

00:45:37.730 --> 00:45:38.630
50 years.

00:45:38.630 --> 00:45:40.950
But it still works.

00:45:40.950 --> 00:45:42.200
Well, that's terrific.

00:45:42.200 --> 00:45:44.330
Thank you so much,
John, on behalf

00:45:44.330 --> 00:45:47.400
of the MIT Alumni Association
for joining us here today.

00:45:47.400 --> 00:45:49.730
This has been a
terrific conversation.

00:45:49.730 --> 00:45:53.300
And thanks to the alumni for
tuning in for the questions,

00:45:53.300 --> 00:45:54.290
and for the questions.

00:45:54.290 --> 00:45:56.220
If we did not get
to your question,

00:45:56.220 --> 00:46:02.060
we will pass it on to Professor
Harbison after the webcast.

00:46:02.060 --> 00:46:05.330
And you can also share other
reactions or further questions

00:46:05.330 --> 00:46:07.250
using hashtag #MITBetterWorld.

